RAWMSTAT(1)

# NAME

rawmstat - native status acquisition service for rawm

# SYNOPSIS

*rawmstat* [*-p*] [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-C* [*-c* _path_]

*rawmstat* *-v*

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmstat* acquires named status sources and publishes an ordered semantic
snapshot for *rawm*(1). It is a separate process: rawm owns presentation and
interaction while rawmstat owns acquisition, sampling state, freshness, and
publication.

The snapshot is published as the *\_RAWM\_STATUS\_V3* property of the X root
window with type *UTF8_STRING*. Each record contains a stable identifier, one
of the states *normal*, *warning*, *critical*, or *stale*, and printable UTF-8
text. The complete property is bounded to 4096 bytes and is replaced atomically.

Configuration describes native sources rather than command blocks. CPU and
network deltas are kept in the daemon's memory; no sampling state files are
used. The *exec* provider is an extension boundary for measurements without a
native provider and is not the status scheduling model.

Unless *-p* is used, rawmstat requires an X display. It does not start or
supervise *rawm*(1); *rawmsession*(1) normally owns both process lifetimes.

# OPTIONS

*-c* _path_
	Load built-in defaults followed by exactly _path_. Normal system and user
	configuration files are not read.

*-C*
	Load and validate the effective configuration, then exit without opening
	the X display or acquiring sources.

*-p*
	Write each changed rawm-v3 snapshot to standard output instead of the X root
	property. A blank line terminates each published snapshot.

*-v*
	Print the version and exit.

# RUNTIME MODEL

Each source has one provider and a monotonic *period_ms*. Native providers read
system interfaces directly and retain provider state in the long-running
process. A successful refresh replaces the source value and semantic state.

If a refresh fails after a source has produced a valid value, that value is
retained but its state becomes *stale*. A source that has never produced a valid
value is omitted. The next successful refresh clears *stale*.

The initial native providers are *battery*, *clock*, *cpu*, *filesystem*,
*memory*, and *network*. The bounded *exec* provider directly executes an argv
vector with *execvp*(3), consumes its first output line, and accepts an optional
leading *normal<TAB>*, *warning<TAB>*, or *critical<TAB>* semantic marker.
*stale* cannot be supplied by a command because freshness belongs to rawmstat.

# SIGNALS

*SIGINT*, *SIGTERM*
	Terminate cleanly.

*SIGHUP*
	Reload the selected configuration. An invalid replacement is rejected and
	the running configuration remains authoritative.

Realtime update signals from the former block architecture are not supported.

# FILES

_/etc/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	System configuration, when present.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is set.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmstat.conf_
	User configuration fallback.

# EXIT STATUS

*0* on normal termination or successful configuration validation; *>0* on a
configuration, display, setup, or publication error.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm*(1), *rawmsession*(1), *rawm.conf*(5), *rawmstat.conf*(5), *libconfig*(5)

